{
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "gdguard",
          "version": "<version>",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/GDQuest/GDScript-formatter",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "duplicated-load",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The same resource path is loaded more than once in a file."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Loading the same path in several places repeats the string and makes renames error-prone. Load it once into a constant and reuse it."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "fixable": false,
                "origin": "builtin"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "max-line-length",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Lines are no longer than the configured maximum (100 by default)."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Long lines are hard to read side by side and in diffs. The limit comes from `max-line-length.max` in gdguard.toml, then `max_line_length` in .editorconfig, then 100."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "fixable": false,
                "origin": "builtin"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "missing-class-name",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Every project script declares a class_name, except autoloads."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A class_name lets other scripts use the script as a type, which enables typed code and the cross-file checks. Autoload scripts are exempt: they are reached through their singleton name. The unsafe fix adds a PascalCase name from the file name when it is free."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "fixable": true,
                "origin": "builtin"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "naming-convention",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Declarations follow GDScript naming conventions."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Combines class, signal, enum, enum-member, constant, variable, loaded-resource variable, function, argument and loop-variable naming. Classes and enums use PascalCase, constants and enum members CONSTANT_CASE, and other names snake_case with private prefixes where appropriate. Loaded resources also allow PascalCase. Renames require checking references, so no automatic fix is offered. Old check names remain accepted in config and ignores."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "fixable": false,
                "origin": "builtin"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "no-else-return",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "No `else`/`elif` after an `if` block that ends with `return`."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "When the `if` branch returns, the code after it only runs in the other case anyway. Removing the `else` reduces nesting."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "fixable": true,
                "origin": "builtin"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "no-get-node-in-process",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Don't look up nodes in `$FUNC()`; cache the node in an `@onready` variable"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`_process()` and `_physics_process()` run every frame. Looking a node up by\npath each time costs time and hides the dependency. An `@onready` variable\nlooks it up once, when the node is ready, and documents what the script\nneeds. Unique names (`%Name`) are cached by Godot and are not reported.\nThere is no automatic fix: the variable has to be added at the top of the\nscript, which is outside the reported code."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "fixable": false,
                "origin": "custom"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "prefer-is-empty",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `$X.is_empty()` instead of comparing `len($X)` to zero"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Comparing `len()` to zero hides the intent of the check. `is_empty()` reads\nas a question about the collection and doesn't count its elements.\nSee also `prefer-not-is-empty` for `len(x) > 0` and `len(x) != 0`."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "fixable": true,
                "origin": "custom"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "prefer-is-empty-call",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Emptiness checks on arrays, dictionaries and strings use is_empty()."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`items.is_empty()` states the intent better than `items.size() > 0` or `len(items) == 0`. Only identifiers whose type is declared (or set from a literal like `[]`) as an Array, Dictionary, packed array or String are reported, so the safe fix never changes behavior."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "fixable": true,
                "origin": "builtin"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "prefer-not-is-empty",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use `not $X.is_empty()` instead of comparing `len($X)` to zero"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`len(x) > 0` and `len(x) != 0` ask whether a collection has elements by\ncounting them. `not x.is_empty()` asks the question directly.\nThis is the counterpart of `prefer-is-empty`; a rule has one fix template,\nso the two checks are separate rules."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "fixable": true,
                "origin": "custom"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "prefer-uid-path",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "load() and preload() use uid:// paths when the resource has a UID."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "UID paths keep working when files are moved or renamed. Only paths with a known UID (from `.uid` files, `.import` files or scene headers) are reported, and the safe fix replaces the path with the UID."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "fixable": true,
                "origin": "builtin"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "private-access",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Members starting with `_` are not used from outside their class."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A leading underscore marks a member as private. Using it from another object couples the two classes to internals that may change."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "fixable": false,
                "origin": "builtin"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "redundant-expression",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Expressions have an effect and don't compare or assign a value to itself."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Combines comparison-with-itself, self-assignment and standalone-expression. These commonly indicate a typo, but the intended correction cannot be inferred. Member self-assignment is exempt because it may invoke a setter. Old check names remain accepted in config and ignores."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "fixable": false,
                "origin": "builtin"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "scene-connection-missing-method",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Scene connections target methods the script defines."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A `[connection]` in a `.tscn` file names its method as a string. Godot only reports a missing method when the signal is emitted. Methods inherited from engine classes can't be checked, except that engine methods never start with `_` unless they are virtual callbacks."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "fixable": false,
                "origin": "builtin"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "type-annotations",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Functions, parameters, variables, constants, signals and containers declare types."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Explicit types document contracts and let Godot check them. Includes function and lambda return types (including void), parameters, variables, constants, for-loop variables, signal parameters and Array/Dictionary arguments. Property accessors inherit their property's type. Explicit Variant is allowed for intentional dynamic values. With allow_inferred = true, := declarations/default parameters and implicit loop-variable types are accepted without proving their inferred type. Types are never guessed or inserted automatically."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "fixable": false,
                "origin": "builtin"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "unused-code",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Variables, arguments, private functions and signals are used; redundant pass is removed."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Combines unused variables, arguments, assigned-but-never-read values, private functions, local/project signals and unnecessary-pass. Project signal checks include script and scene connections, with a local fallback outside a project. Fix safety is per diagnostic: redundant pass removal is safe, variable removal and argument renaming unsafe, private-function removal suggestion-only; other checks have no fix. Old check names remain accepted in config, ignores, unsafe-fix allowlists and baselines."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "fixable": true,
                "origin": "builtin"
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      },
